.TH WATCHIP 1 "2026-04-14" "watchip 2.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
watchip \- monitor and log public IP address changes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B watchip
[\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B watchip log
.br
.B source watchip
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B watchip
compares the current public IP (via
.BR internetip (1))
against a stored value and logs state changes. It is designed for
unattended cron execution and writes to syslog (facility
.BR local0.notice )
when run as root.
.PP
On first run,
.B watchip
fetches the IP, writes it to
.I $IPFILE
(default
.IR /tmp/watchip.txt ),
and records an
.B "Initial IP"
log entry.
On subsequent runs it compares stored vs. current; on a change it rewrites
.I $IPFILE
and records an
.B "IP changed from X to Y"
log entry. When the IP is unchanged and
.B \-q
is supplied (typical for cron), the tool is completely silent.
.PP
When sourced as a Bash module,
.B watchip
exports
.B watch_ip
and lazy-loads its
.BR internetip (1)
dependency on first call, so sourced callers never pollute their own
shell namespace with
.BR internetip 's
top-level helpers.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-log ", " log
Display the current contents of
.I $LOGFILE
and exit. The
.B log
form is a positional subcommand alias.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress the
.B "IP unchanged"
message. Change events and errors are still printed.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display version and exit.
.SH EXPORTED FUNCTION
When sourced, the following function becomes available:
.TP
.BI "watch_ip " "[ipfile]"
Compare the IP stored in
.I ipfile
(default
.IR /tmp/internetip.txt )
against the current public IP. Prints one of:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BI initial: ip
\- first run, no
.I ipfile
yet.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI changed: old : new
\- the IP has changed.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI unchanged: ip
\- the IP is the same as stored.
.RE
.IP
Returns 18
.RB ( ERR_NODEP )
if
.BR internetip (1)
is not available, or 1 if the IP fetch fails.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LOGFILE
Log file path. Default:
.IR /var/log/watchip.log .
If that path is not writable at startup,
.B watchip
falls back to
.IR "~/.watchip.log" .
.TP
.B IPFILE
IP tracking file. Default:
.IR /tmp/watchip.txt .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B watchip
uses the BCS0602 canonical exit-code vocabulary:
.TP
.B 0
Success \- IP checked (initial, changed, or unchanged).
.TP
.B 18
ERR_NODEP \-
.BR internetip (1)
dependency unreachable.
.TP
.B 22
ERR_INVAL \- unknown option or unexpected argument. These errors are
written to stderr only; they do not pollute the operational log.
.TP
.B 23
ERR_NETWORK \- failed to fetch or validate the current IP.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/log/watchip.log
Default operational log file (when writable by the invoking user).
.TP
.I ~/.watchip.log
Fallback log file when
.I /var/log/watchip.log
is not writable.
.TP
.I /tmp/watchip.txt
IP tracking file (default).
.SH EXAMPLES
Run from a terminal and display result:
.PP
.EX
watchip
.EE
.PP
Quiet mode, suitable for cron:
.PP
.EX
*/5 * * * * /usr/local/bin/watchip -q
.EE
.PP
Display the operational log:
.PP
.EX
watchip --log
watchip -l
watchip log
.EE
.PP
Use as a Bash module with a custom tracking file:
.PP
.EX
source watchip
result=$(watch_ip /tmp/myapp_ip.txt)
case $result in
  initial:*)   echo "First run: ${result#initial:}" ;;
  changed:*)   echo "IP changed" ;;
  unchanged:*) echo "IP unchanged" ;;
esac
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR internetip (1),
.BR validip (1),
.BR logger (1),
.BR crontab (5),
.BR syslog (3)
.SH AUTHOR
Biksu Okusi <biksu@okusi.id>
